# Build context exclusions for the API image.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS — this was a real, verified defect, not hygiene.
#
# backend/Dockerfile does `COPY . .`. With no .dockerignore that copied
# `backend/.env` — a developer's real local environment file — into the
# image. A pre-launch deployment check found the built image carrying a
# live OPENAI_API_KEY, plus a SECRET_KEY and a DATABASE_URL, in its
# layers. Anyone able to pull or inspect the image could read them
# (`docker history`, or just extracting the layer).
#
# It was not merely disclosed, it was in USE. docker-compose.yml passes
# OPENAI_API_KEY as a bare passthrough, deliberately, so that an unset
# host variable does not shadow a developer's local config. That means an
# unset variable in production fell through to the value baked into the
# image: a production deployment would have quietly run inference on a
# developer's personal key. A smoke test against a production-configured
# stack with no OPENAI_API_KEY on the host returned a real model
# completion, which is how this was found.
#
# It also contradicted the project's own rule, DEPLOYMENT.md §2:
# "Secrets must never appear in source, Git, the Docker image, the
# frontend bundle, documentation, or logs."
#
# frontend/.dockerignore already excluded .env.local; the backend simply
# never got the equivalent.
#
# Local development is unaffected: docker-compose.dev.yml bind-mounts
# ./backend over /app, so backend/.env is present at runtime from the
# host. Production now has no fallback, which is the point — a missing
# key fails visibly instead of silently using the wrong one.

# ─── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The actual defect. .env.example is a template with no real values and
# is safe, but there is no reason to ship it either.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ─── Python build artefacts ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Bytecode compiled on a developer's machine has no business in the
# image; Python regenerates it. These were tracked by mistake once
# already (see .gitignore).
# Note the `**/` prefixes: .dockerignore is NOT .gitignore. Docker matches
# with Go's filepath rules, where a bare `__pycache__/` matches only the
# context root, not nested directories. Without `**/` these patterns
# silently did nothing and the bytecode shipped anyway.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info
**/.pytest_cache

# ─── Local runtime state ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# prometheus_client multiprocess files. In production these live in the
# tmpfs at PROMETHEUS_MULTIPROC_DIR and are created at boot; a stale one
# copied in from a developer's machine would be counted as a live worker.
counter_*.db
gauge_livesum_*.db
histogram_*.db

# A Windows-absolute PROMETHEUS_MULTIPROC_DIR hitting a POSIX path join
# once created a literal `C:/...` tree inside backend/. Same rule as
# .gitignore — nothing legitimate is ever named this.
[A-Za-z]:/

# ─── Version control / editor noise ───────────────────────────────────
.git
.gitignore
# Glob, not a literal name — and `**/`-prefixed for the same reason the
# __pycache__ patterns above are: Docker matches with Go's filepath
# rules, where a bare `venv/` matches only the context root, not a
# nested directory. A differently-named local virtualenv (e.g. one an
# ad-hoc audit/tooling session creates) is not excluded by a literal
# `.venv/` either. Verified the hard way during the 2026-09 security
# gate — a throwaway `.venv-audit/` used to run the test suite outside
# Docker got copied into a real `docker build` of this image, because
# only `.venv/` and `venv/` were listed. Same class of defect as the
# `.env`-in-image incident this file already exists to prevent — see the
# header above — just for build tooling instead of secrets.
**/.venv/
**/.venv*/
**/venv/
**/*venv/
